Current original Zero workflow (0.12)

Use the native tools on Linux or their .exe versions on Windows:
  zero-integrate PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-build PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_OUTPUT_DIRECTORY NEW_KERNEL.zero
  zero-check PROJECT_DIRECTORY NEW_TEST_DIRECTORY
All destination paths must be new. The integrator preserves authored sources;
the builder checks its compiler fixed point and builds the original targets;
the verifier checks language behavior and host file operations in process.
See tools/integrate.ztext, tools/build.ztext and checks/README.ztext.
Read these documents with zero-text read FILE.

The notes below preserve earlier implementation and test history. Any old
shell/Python runner names, generated artifacts, JSON reports or commands are
historical references: they were removed from the active project and are not
current runnable instructions or current-release validation. Native host
checks do not replace BIOS/UEFI, persistence or physical-hardware testing.

Historical implementation notes

Zero NTFS backend: original resident-file implementation

fs/ntfs.zero is original Zero source. Entry300 follows fs/ABI.ztext and reloads
the volume on every operation. The same source is used in the monolithic
kernel and the hosted verification harness; no ntfs-3g/Linux/Windows driver
code is linked or invoked by Zero OS. The compiler emits native machine code
directly. External ntfs-3g programs are only disposable-image fixture makers
and independent validators.

Implemented operations
  probe; watch root directory; read resident file; create empty file;
  save/create-or-replace resident file; remove file.
  Names: flat ASCII letters/digits/dot/underscore/hyphen,1..31 bytes;
  root '.'/'..' and trailing-dot names are refused. Lookup uses standard ASCII
  NTFS case folding; dotfiles are listed. hiberfil.sys is reserved. System names beginning '$' cannot
  be passed through the public filename grammar.
  Data:0..400 bytes. No external data clusters are allocated.

Writable on-disk profile
  NTFS3.1,512-byte sectors,4096-byte clusters,1024-byte FILE records and
  4096-byte INDX records. Volume must be clean and have no hiberfil.sys.
  Both first4096-byte $LogFile restart pages must be unused formatter bytes
  (all0xff). Initialized Windows journals are explicitly unsupported even
  when VolumeDirty is clear, because this backend cannot prove no replay is
  needed. $LogFile must have one ordinary data extent covering these pages.
  $MFT data and bitmap, $MFTMirr data and root $I30 allocation must each have
  one ordinary positive mapping pair. Attribute lists, sparse, compressed,
  encrypted or named data streams are refused. Files must have one resident
  FILE_NAME, one resident ordinary DATA attribute, one hard link, and standard
  archive metadata; a security-descriptor attribute may be preserved.
  Root index is a one-child INDEX_ROOT pointing to one existing leaf INDX.
  The backend does not split index nodes, grow index allocation, allocate
  data clusters, grow the MFT allocation, or grow the MFT bitmap. The MFT
  bitmap is at most512 bytes, supporting at most4096 existing MFT slots.
  Create uses a free existing MFT slot numbered24 or above. It may initialize
  the immediately following record within already allocated MFT space and
  update the MFT's initialized/data sizes, FILE_NAME cache and root index key.
  A fresh64MiB mkntfs fixture has only one available slot under this bounded
  profile. Volumes with a larger existing MFT have more slots. Exhaustion
  returns capacity without changing the image; deleting a file frees its slot.
  New files inherit valid standard-information timestamps and security ID
  from the existing MFT metadata. There is no wall-clock timestamp service
  or Windows ACL inheritance implementation in this backend.

Validation and write protocol
  Each operation checks signatures, volume bounds, attribute lengths/types,
  FILE/INDX update-sequence arrays, root index entry lengths and ASCII order,
  root reference allocation, disjoint metadata regions including the complete
  allocated $LogFile extent, and MFT/MFTMirr
  records0/3. The mirror's data mapping must match the boot sector location.
  Cached FILE_NAME sizes can legally be stale on externally written NTFS;
  a successful save refreshes both FILE_NAME and directory-index size caches.
  Unsupported features, missing files, duplicate creates, bad arguments,
  capacity exhaustion and detected corruption are refused before the first
  write. Entire changes are staged in private buffers before dirtying disk.
  Write transaction: set volume dirty in MFT and mirror, flush; write FILE,
  MFT bitmap, required MFT size/mirror updates and index; flush; clear dirty
  in both volume records; flush before returning success. Remove unlinks the
  index and flushes before freeing the FILE/MFT bitmap allocation.
  Any I/O failure returns failure. Interrupted transactions may leave a dirty
  volume requiring an external recovery/checking tool. There is no NTFS
  $LogFile replay/transaction writer, rollback, atomic multi-sector commit,
  general-purpose NTFS recovery, or claim of production NTFS compatibility.
  Do not represent this bounded experimental implementation as full NTFS.

Reproduction
  bash verification/ntfs/check.sh
  Requires the project's own bootstrap/zero and an installed private copy of
  ntfs-3g fixture/checking tools on PATH, or set ZERO_NTFS_TOOLS_ROOT to a
  private extracted package root. The script recreates only verification/ntfs
  images. It never formats a physical disk or the user's original data images.
  It checks CRUD,400-byte data,31-byte names,case lookup,dotfiles,record reuse,
  independent ntfscat/ntfsls/ntfsinfo/ntfsfix -n interoperability,12 failed
  write/flush positions and11 malformed/unsupported profiles; refused operations compare
  whole-image SHA256 before/after and report zero writes.
  Raw/UEFI x86-64 and IA32 use the same source with the kernel's ATA adapter.
  A successful hosted test is not by itself a claim that a VM boot test ran.

